# TODO - CUDA Linux runtime and hermetic toolchain

## Objective

Port the exact CUDA runtime and repository-local development toolchains to Linux
while preserving platform-specific ABI loading, exact package identity, CPU
fallback, verifier authority, and existing Windows support.

## Acceptance

- Windows selects `ctypes.WinDLL`, `nvcuda.dll`, and its versioned NVRTC `.dll`;
  Linux selects `ctypes.CDLL`, `libcuda.so.1`, and a manifest-selected versioned
  NVRTC `.so` through one reviewed platform loader contract.
- Generic CUDA execution logic contains no hardcoded `13.3.1` toolkit path or
  Windows library suffix; the selected platform manifest owns release and
  library
  identity.
- Windows and Linux manifests pin platform, release, package versions, archive
  paths, sizes, toolkit roots, and SHA-256 values without a "latest" fallback.
- Shared Rust, Jig, LLVM, Python, and validation configuration no longer assumes
  a
  Windows target or `Scripts/*.exe/*.cmd` path on Linux.
- Linux missing-driver, missing-library, platform-mismatch, cleanup, and CPU
  fallback routes fail explicitly without changing semantic acceptance.
- Linux x86-64 exact CPU/CUDA differential tests and retained live-device
  evidence
  exist for every support or performance claim, while existing Windows gates
  pass.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder or undocumented
  workaround
  required for the objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove
  the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Linux CUDA runtime support is now live on the current Fedora x86-64 host.
  Platform selection preserves the byte-exact Windows manifest while adding a
  separate Linux CUDA 13.3.1 manifest. Windows keeps `WinDLL`/`nvcuda.dll`;
  Linux uses `CDLL`/`libcuda.so.1`, repository-local NVRTC, and an explicit
  tracked `libnvrtc-builtins.so.13.3` preload because NVIDIA's NVRTC ELF carries
  no runtime search path to its companion builtins library.
- Explicit `--provision-cuda` bootstrap materializes only the selected tracked
  redistributables after exact size/SHA-256 validation. Ordinary bootstrap does
  not download CUDA. The Linux NVRTC 13.3.33 package is pinned at 69,662,780
  archive bytes with SHA-256
  `9e8f78278215babd1236b137252424ca7912c185bd093201f5d97f7dd763b74a`;
  completion is marker-bound and repeated provisioning reuses the admitted
  toolkit without another download.
- Live support evidence at commit `bc2ebf7b` binds an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4060
  (`sm_89`), display driver `610.57.04`, Driver API `13030`, NVRTC `13.3`,
  CPython `3.14.6`, and Linux manifest SHA-256
  `deaa908864ba3e3f85def6e983aa66d3d30892598423feddb9e9a006bd0491a7`.
  The broad CUDA/runtime/bootstrap slice passes 185 tests with five explicit
  Windows-retained-evidence skips; a separate representative exact CPU/CUDA
  differential set passes 113 tests. No Linux performance claim is made and
  retained Windows ticket-routing timings are never generalized to Fedora.
- Windows manifest SHA-256 remains
  `b8249cc1accf4b0532779c7c42e6505c9840d7208b4ab945e54daa456206b95e`;
  deterministic Windows loader tests remain active even when live Windows
  hardware is unavailable on the Linux host.
- Remaining P0 blocker: shared LLVM/Clang 22.1.8 validation paths still encode
  Windows executable names such as `clang.exe`, `clang-tidy.exe`, and
  `clang-format.exe`. Fedora already supplies exact Clang 22.1.8, but it has not
  yet been admitted through a repository-local platform-neutral LLVM toolchain
  contract. The P0 remains active until that shared validation/toolchain surface
  stops assuming Windows and the final exhaustive Jig gate passes with the
  admitted Linux paths.
- Required durable artifacts: platform loader tests, Linux CUDA package
  manifest, native toolchain resolution, exact fallback tests, and retained
  Linux device evidence.
- Foundation completion evidence: `linux-development-host-bootstrap` and
  `documentation-readiness-and-implementation-gate`. The active
  `cuda-exact-vm-adapter` supplies the implementation surface being ported but
  is not a completion prerequisite for Linux platform enablement.
